1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,102
Gummy now draws charts. Ten types, from bars to gauges, and every one of them is configuration.

2
00:00:08,052 --> 00:00:14,193
A chart reads its rows from an endpoint. The dashed target comes from the same response, read by path.

3
00:00:14,093 --> 00:00:18,957
Click a bar, and that week becomes page data. The block beside it reads it back.

4
00:00:18,857 --> 00:00:23,901
Lines and stacked areas take the same rows, in the app's own date and number formats.

5
00:00:24,351 --> 00:00:31,168
Parts of a whole: a donut with its total, ranked bars, and the order funnel. The legend toggles any slice.

6
00:00:31,068 --> 00:00:37,428
And a click can navigate. This slice opens its genre's books, the clicked row filling the page's URL.

7
00:00:37,878 --> 00:00:42,145
Bars and a line share one chart, with the orders on a right-hand axis.

8
00:00:43,711 --> 00:00:49,642
Every book as a bubble: price against rating, sized by its pages, one series per genre.

9
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:56,697
A heatmap shows when the orders come in, on the theme's accent ramp.

10
00:00:56,597 --> 00:01:01,250
Radars compare the genres on five scores, as a polygon or a circle.

11
00:01:04,289 --> 00:01:09,732
Gauges hold each KPI against its target, with colour bands, radial or linear.

12
00:01:09,632 --> 00:01:15,318
And every chart follows the theme, into dark mode and back, on a palette checked for contrast.

13
00:01:19,128 --> 00:01:25,385
In Gummy Studio, a chart is one item. Pick a type, and the canvas redraws it over sample data.

14
00:01:29,195 --> 00:01:35,562
Series, axes, reference lines, the legend and clicks are all settings, with nothing to code.

15
00:01:36,262 --> 00:01:40,633
Gummy UI. Configure the chart, and keep your code for the business.
